            <ArticleTitle>Intramuscular migration of calcific tendinopathy in the rotator cuff: ultrasound appearance and a review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>175-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s40477-016-0202-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Calcific tendinopathy of the shoulder is a common condition caused by calcium hydroxyapatite crystals, affecting the tendons of the rotator cuff. Among uncommon complication, one is the migration of the calcium in the subacromion-subdeltoid bursa. More rare is the intraosseous migration. We present four cases of an even more rare condition, not well described in literature yet, the intramuscular migration of calcium.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
